<%#
  One page: who charges the customer, the keys they need, and who in your
  application is allowed to touch billing.

  NO PROCESSOR IS NAMED IN THIS FILE. The dropdown is whatever Pay is willing
  to run, and the fields under it are whatever Pay reads for the one chosen --
  both asked of Pay at render time. A page listing them by hand would be right
  on the day it was written and wrong at Pay's next release, and nothing would
  say so. See lib/sparrow_pay/console/processors.rb.

  These are console pages and Tailwind's `@source` scans this directory by
  name. Views for the console belong here and nowhere else.
%>
<h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Payments</h1>
<p class="mt-1 text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  SparrowKit bills the <span class="font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">organization</span>,
  never the person &mdash; somebody can belong to several and can leave, and a
  subscription attached to a person walks out of the door with them. What each
  plan costs is configured at your processor, not here.
</p>

<% unless @writable %>
  <div role="alert"
       class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-900 dark:bg-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
    <p class="font-medium">This page cannot save anything yet</p>
    <p class="mt-1"><%= @not_writable_reason %></p>
  </div>
<% end %>

<form id="pay_form" action="<%= sparrowkit_path %>" method="post" autocomplete="off" class="mt-8">
  <%#
    Written out rather than built by form_with. Tailwind only compiles classes
    it can find in a file, and a class inside an ERB tag is invisible both to
    the build's scanner and to the spec that checks the build is not stale.
  %>
  <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="patch" autocomplete="off">
  <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>" autocomplete="off">

  <section class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Processor</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Who takes the money. Pay can talk to several and will not pick one for
      you &mdash; until this is set, no organization can be given a customer
      record and no checkout can start.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <label for="pay_processor" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
        Charge through
      </label>
      <select id="pay_processor"
              name="processor"
              onchange="this.form.requestSubmit ? this.form.requestSubmit() : this.form.submit()"
              class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-900 focus:border-purple-900 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-purple-900 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:focus:border-purple-300 dark:focus:ring-purple-300">
        <option value="">Not set</option>
        <% @processors.each do |name| %>
          <option value="<%= name %>" <%= "selected" if @processor == name %>>
            <%= SparrowPay::Console::Processors.label_for(name) %>
          </option>
        <% end %>
      </select>
      <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-300">
        Changing this reloads the page, because each processor asks for
        different keys. Nothing is saved until you press Save.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <% if @processor %>
    <section class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
        <%= SparrowPay::Console::Processors.label_for(@processor) %> keys
      </h2>

      <% if @client_gem_missing %>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Pay supports this processor but its client library is not installed,
          so there is nothing to fill in yet. Add the gem to your Gemfile,
          <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">bundle install</code>,
          restart, and its fields appear here.
        </p>
      <% else %>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          These are written to
          <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800"><%= @processor %>:</code>
          in your credentials, which is where Pay reads them. The settings below
          go to
          <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">sparrow_pay:</code>,
          which is where this gem reads its own.
        </p>

        <% @fields.each do |field| %>
          <% optional = SparrowPay::Console::Processors.optional?(field[:name]) %>
          <% boolean = SparrowPay::Console::Processors.boolean?(field[:name]) %>
          <div class="mt-4">
            <%#
              A checkbox wears its label to the RIGHT, on the same line, because
              that is where a checkbox's label goes: the two read as one control
              and the whole phrase is the hit target. Above it, the label sat
              over an unattached square and the pair read as a heading with an
              orphan under it.

              Every other field keeps its label above, which is where a label
              belongs when the control is a box you type into.
            %>
            <% if boolean %>
              <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                <%#
                  Hidden field first, so the parameter is always present.
                  A checkbox alone sends nothing when it is off, and for THIS
                  field absent is not the same as off: Pay reads a blank value
                  as TRUE (`value.blank? || cast(value)`), so an unchecked box
                  that sent nothing would be stored as blank and come back on.
                  The false is what makes off mean off.
                %>
                <input type="hidden" name="<%= field[:name] %>" value="false" autocomplete="off">
                <input type="checkbox"
                       id="pay_<%= field[:name] %>"
                       name="<%= field[:name] %>"
                       value="true"
                       <%= "checked" if field[:value].to_s == "true" %>
                       class="size-4 shrink-0 rounded border-slate-500 text-purple-900 dark:border-slate-600 dark:text-purple-300">
                <%#
                  No tick on a checkbox. The tick means "a value is stored", and
                  for a switch that is true whether it was stored on or off --
                  so a saved `false` drew a tick beside an empty box, which
                  reads as "this is on" and is the one thing it is not.
                %>
                <label for="pay_<%= field[:name] %>" class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"><%= SparrowPay::Console::Processors.label_for(field[:name]) %><%= render "sparrow_ui/console/optional" if optional %></label>
              </div>
            <% else %>
            <%#
              "Optional" is the word doing the most work on this page. Pay
              exposes every credential the same way, with no way to ask which
              are required, so before this the card was five identical boxes and
              a developer filled in all five -- hunting for two that do not
              exist for an ordinary account.
            %>
            <label for="pay_<%= field[:name] %>" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"><%= SparrowPay::Console::Processors.label_for(field[:name]) %><%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if field[:set] %><%= render "sparrow_ui/console/optional" if optional %></label>
            <%#
              A secret goes through sparrow_ui's field, which is a text box
              masked by CSS rather than a `type="password"`. That is what stops
              the browser offering to save a processor's API key to the
              developer's password manager -- see the partial for why
              `autocomplete="off"` cannot do it.
            %>
            <% if field[:secret] %>
              <%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_field",
                    id: "pay_#{field[:name]}",
                    name: field[:name],
                    hint: (field[:hint] if field[:set]) %>
            <% else %>
            <input type="text"
                   id="pay_<%= field[:name] %>"
                   name="<%= field[:name] %>"
                   value="<%= field[:value] %>"
                   autocomplete="off"
                   spellcheck="false"
                   class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 focus:border-purple-900 focus:outline-none focus:ring-1 focus:ring-purple-900 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:focus:border-purple-300 dark:focus:ring-purple-300">
            <% end %>
            <% end %>
            <% if (help = SparrowPay::Console::Processors.help_for(field[:name])) %>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300"><%= help %></p>
            <% end %>

            <% if field[:secret] %>
              <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                <%#
                  No longer repeats the last four characters. The tick beside
                  the label says a key is stored and the box shows which one,
                  so saying it a third time in prose was the noise that made
                  the first two hard to read.
                %>
                <% if field[:set] %>
                  Leave blank to keep it; type a new one to replace it.
                <% else %>
                  Not set. Pay reads this from your credentials.
                <% end %>
              </p>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </section>
  <% end %>

  <%#
    Two cards where there was one called "Access".

    It held the permissions class AND the address a customer is returned to
    after paying, and the second of those is not access by any reading. A title
    that describes half its card is a title somebody stops reading.
  %>
  <%#
    THERE IS NO BOX HERE ANY MORE, and the absence is the point.

    This card asked for the name of a class implementing the per-record policy
    pattern for billing. That pattern went in 0.1.0 along with the billing pages it guarded,
    so what was left was a text box asking for a class that could not be written,
    protecting pages that no longer existed, with an amber warning that until it
    was filled in "the billing pages refuse everybody".
  %>
  <section class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Who may see and change billing</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Nothing to set here, because it is the same question as everything else:
      billing belongs to an organization, and who may read or change it is
      decided by the role somebody holds in that organization.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      In your own controller, where you can read and test it:
    </p>
    <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded-md bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"><code>require_organization!(permission: "billing:read")
require_organization!(permission: "billing:manage")</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Both permissions ship: <code class="font-mono">billing:read</code> is held from
      admin upwards and <code class="font-mono">billing:manage</code> by owners.
      Reading which plan you are on and changing the card are deliberately not
      the same permission.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">After paying</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Where your customer lands when they finish at the processor &mdash; after a
      successful payment, after cancelling one, and after closing the billing
      portal. All three come back to the same page, which then reads what
      happened and says so.
    </p>

  </section>

  <section class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Development</h2>

    <div class="mt-3 flex items-start gap-3">
      <%#
        Hidden field first, so the parameter is always present. A checkbox alone
        sends nothing when it is off, and "absent" would then be the same
        request as "the client never rendered the control".
      %>
      <input type="hidden" name="allow_test_processor" value="0" autocomplete="off">
      <input type="checkbox"
             id="pay_allow_test_processor"
             name="allow_test_processor"
             value="1"
             <%= "checked" if @allow_fake %>
             class="mt-0.5 size-4 rounded border-slate-400 text-purple-900 focus:ring-purple-900 dark:border-slate-600 dark:text-purple-300 dark:focus:ring-purple-300">
      <span>
        <label for="pay_allow_test_processor" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
          Allow Pay's test processor
        </label>
        <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-300">
          Lets subscriptions be created with no payment provider behind them, for
          tests and local work. Off unless you say otherwise, deliberately: an
          application that fell back to it would take no money and say nothing
          about it.
        </span>
      </span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-6 flex items-center gap-3">
    <button type="submit"
            <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
            class="rounded-md bg-purple-900 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-purple-800 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-purple-900 focus:ring-offset-2 disabled:cursor-not-allowed disabled:opacity-50 dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950 dark:hover:bg-purple-200">
      Save
    </button>
    <a href="<%= request.script_name.sub(%r{/pay\z}, "") %>"
       class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-600 hover:underline dark:text-slate-300">Back to SparrowKit</a>
  </div>
</form>

<h2 class="mt-10 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Not configured here</h2>
<ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">Plans, prices and what they cost</span> &mdash; those live at your
    processor, where you already configured them. Restating them here would be
    two places to change and a guarantee they drift.
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">What a plan unlocks</span> &mdash; your processor has never heard of
    your feature names. Ask
    <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">organization.billing.active?</code>
    and gate on it yourself.
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">Webhooks</span> &mdash; Pay receives and verifies them. Point your
    processor at
    <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">/pay/webhooks/&lt;processor&gt;</code>
    and the copied columns keep themselves current.
  </li>
</ul>
